1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,508
An onion peel.

2
00:00:01,508 --> 00:00:03,670
A scraping from inside your cheek.

3
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:05,168
A drop of pond water.

4
00:00:05,568 --> 00:00:10,117
Put any of them under a microscope,
and you see tiny units, packed together

5
00:00:10,117 --> 00:00:11,273
or moving about.

6
00:00:11,723 --> 00:00:13,637
Diya saw this in the college lab.

7
00:00:13,637 --> 00:00:17,202
She asked: why is every living
thing built from these tiny units?

8
00:00:17,202 --> 00:00:18,942
And who first worked this out?

9
00:00:19,292 --> 00:00:20,193
Two answers.

10
00:00:20,193 --> 00:00:23,153
These units are cells,
the basic unit of life.

11
00:00:23,153 --> 00:00:26,967
And it took many scientists,
one after another, to prove it.

12
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:31,703
By the end, you will know who first
saw cells, the cell theory and its

13
00:00:31,703 --> 00:00:35,128
two rules, and how much cells
differ in size and shape.

14
00:00:35,778 --> 00:00:37,299
First, what is a cell?

15
00:00:37,649 --> 00:00:39,282
Think of bricks in a wall.

16
00:00:39,282 --> 00:00:42,719
One brick is small, but a whole
building is made of bricks.

17
00:00:43,169 --> 00:00:44,923
A cell is like that brick.

18
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:47,237
Some living things are just one cell.

19
00:00:47,237 --> 00:00:50,543
They are unicellular, and
they can live on their own.

20
00:00:50,543 --> 00:00:53,803
Anything less than a whole
cell cannot live by itself.

21
00:00:53,803 --> 00:00:57,688
So the cell is the basic unit of
structure and function of life.

22
00:00:58,338 --> 00:01:02,778
Let's zoom into an onion peel,
just like the early scientists did.

23
00:01:03,128 --> 00:01:08,049
At low zoom, the peel is a sheet
of tiny boxes, packed side by side.

24
00:01:08,049 --> 00:01:09,707
Each box is one cell.

25
00:01:10,207 --> 00:01:11,701
Zoom into one cell.

26
00:01:11,701 --> 00:01:14,481
Its outer boundary is a firm cell wall.

27
00:01:14,481 --> 00:01:17,043
Just inside it is the cell membrane.

28
00:01:17,043 --> 00:01:20,300
The jelly that fills the
cell is the cytoplasm.

29
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:21,741
Zoom again.

30
00:01:21,741 --> 00:01:25,473
Inside sits a dense, round
body wrapped in a membrane.

31
00:01:25,473 --> 00:01:26,997
This is the nucleus.

32
00:01:26,997 --> 00:01:31,077
It holds chromosomes, which
carry the genetic material, DNA.

33
00:01:31,577 --> 00:01:36,086
Cells with a membrane bound nucleus
like this are called eukaryotic.

34
00:01:36,086 --> 00:01:38,441
Cells without one are prokaryotic.

35
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:42,315
Now the story of the cell
theory, step by step.

36
00:01:42,665 --> 00:01:43,565
Step one.

37
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,928
Anton Von Leeuwenhoek first
saw and described a live cell.

38
00:01:45,928 --> 00:01:47,970
Later, Robert Brown
discovered the nucleus.

39
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:49,620
Step two.

40
00:01:49,413 --> 00:01:53,255
In 1838, Matthias Schleiden, a
German botanist, studied many plants.

41
00:01:53,255 --> 00:01:57,173
He found that all plants are made of
different kinds of cells, which form

42
00:01:57,173 --> 00:01:58,693
the tissues of the plant.

43
00:01:59,443 --> 00:02:00,343
Step three.

44
00:02:00,289 --> 00:02:03,158
In 1839, Theodore Schwann
studied animal cells.

45
00:02:03,158 --> 00:02:07,185
He saw a thin outer layer, which
we now call the plasma membrane.

46
00:02:07,185 --> 00:02:10,697
He also found that a cell wall
is special to plant cells.

47
00:02:11,447 --> 00:02:12,347
Step four.

48
00:02:12,075 --> 00:02:15,316
Schwann said that the bodies of animals
and plants are made of cells and

49
00:02:15,316 --> 00:02:16,276
products of cells.

50
00:02:16,276 --> 00:02:18,789
Together, Schleiden and
Schwann gave the cell theory.

51
00:02:18,789 --> 00:02:21,135
But it did not explain
how new cells are formed.

52
00:02:21,885 --> 00:02:22,785
Step five.

53
00:02:22,567 --> 00:02:26,112
In 1855, Rudolf Virchow explained
that cells divide, and new cells form

54
00:02:26,112 --> 00:02:27,720
from cells that already exist.

55
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:29,610
In Latin, omnis cellula e cellula.

56
00:02:29,610 --> 00:02:31,751
He gave the cell theory its final shape.

57
00:02:32,501 --> 00:02:33,418
Remember this.

58
00:02:33,418 --> 00:02:35,182
Every cell comes from a cell.

59
00:02:35,782 --> 00:02:39,456
So, the cell theory as we know
it today has just two rules.

60
00:02:39,806 --> 00:02:40,706
Rule one.

61
00:02:40,503 --> 00:02:43,872
All living organisms are made
of cells and products of cells.

62
00:02:44,322 --> 00:02:48,261
Products of cells are things that
cells make and leave around them, like

63
00:02:48,261 --> 00:02:50,052
the wall around a plant cell.

64
00:02:50,502 --> 00:02:51,402
Rule two.

65
00:02:51,324 --> 00:02:55,956
All cells arise from pre existing
cells, cells that were already there.

66
00:02:55,956 --> 00:02:57,551
Watch one cell divide.

67
00:02:57,551 --> 00:02:58,944
Now there are two.

68
00:02:59,594 --> 00:03:01,800
Cells come in very different sizes.

69
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:06,696
The smallest cells are Mycoplasmas,
only zero point three micrometres long.

70
00:03:06,696 --> 00:03:09,637
A micrometre is one
thousandth of a millimetre.

71
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:12,558
Bacteria can be three to
five micrometres long.

72
00:03:12,558 --> 00:03:15,525
A human red blood cell is
about seven micrometres across.

73
00:03:15,975 --> 00:03:19,134
The largest single cell
is the egg of an ostrich.

74
00:03:19,134 --> 00:03:22,091
And nerve cells are some
of the longest cells.

75
00:03:22,741 --> 00:03:26,653
Cells also come in many shapes,
and each shape suits its job.

76
00:03:27,003 --> 00:03:31,290
A red blood cell is round and
biconcave, pressed in on both sides.

77
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:33,381
A white blood cell is amoeboid.

78
00:03:33,381 --> 00:03:35,269
It keeps changing its shape.

79
00:03:35,719 --> 00:03:38,159
A nerve cell is long and branched.

80
00:03:38,159 --> 00:03:41,217
Columnar epithelial cells
are long and narrow.

81
00:03:41,217 --> 00:03:46,036
In plants, a tracheid is long, and
mesophyll cells are round or oval.

82
00:03:46,636 --> 00:03:47,536
Your turn.

83
00:03:47,521 --> 00:03:50,858
Which is smaller: a
bacterium, or a Mycoplasma?

84
00:03:50,858 --> 00:03:52,160
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:56,290
The Mycoplasma!

86
00:03:56,290 --> 00:03:58,508
It is only zero point three micrometres.

87
00:03:58,508 --> 00:04:00,218
A bacterium is three to five.

88
00:04:00,868 --> 00:04:02,445
Now, a worked example.

89
00:04:02,445 --> 00:04:05,039
Let's test the cell theory on three cases.

90
00:04:05,389 --> 00:04:06,289
Case one.

91
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:09,421
An Amoeba is a single cell
that lives on its own.

92
00:04:09,421 --> 00:04:11,997
It is unicellular, and rule one holds.

93
00:04:12,447 --> 00:04:13,347
Case two.

94
00:04:13,205 --> 00:04:14,926
A cut on your knee heals.

95
00:04:14,926 --> 00:04:18,164
The new skin cells come from
old skin cells dividing.

96
00:04:18,164 --> 00:04:19,404
That is rule two.

97
00:04:19,854 --> 00:04:20,754
Case three.

98
00:04:20,681 --> 00:04:23,133
Compare an onion cell with a cheek cell.

99
00:04:23,133 --> 00:04:25,127
The onion cell has a cell wall.

100
00:04:25,127 --> 00:04:27,301
The cheek cell has only a membrane.

101
00:04:27,751 --> 00:04:29,189
Just as Schwann found.

102
00:04:29,189 --> 00:04:31,658
The cell wall is special to plant cells.

103
00:04:32,308 --> 00:04:33,770
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:36,541
Many students think Schleiden and
Schwann already explained where new cells

105
00:04:36,541 --> 00:04:37,441
come from.

106
00:04:37,413 --> 00:04:38,573
But they did not.

107
00:04:38,573 --> 00:04:40,618
Rudolf Virchow added it, in 1855.

108
00:04:40,618 --> 00:04:43,156
New cells come only
from pre existing cells.

109
00:04:43,806 --> 00:04:45,134
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:45,134 --> 00:04:49,125
As match or statement questions on
who did what, and on cell sizes.

111
00:04:49,725 --> 00:04:50,870
Exam level now.

112
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:53,159
Match each scientist with the work.

113
00:04:53,159 --> 00:04:54,352
Pause and solve.

114
00:04:59,752 --> 00:05:01,225
The answer is C.

115
00:05:01,225 --> 00:05:04,116
One q, two s, three r, four p.

116
00:05:04,616 --> 00:05:07,448
Schleiden found that all
plants are made of cells.

117
00:05:07,448 --> 00:05:10,142
Schwann saw the plasma
membrane in animal cells.

118
00:05:10,642 --> 00:05:11,988
Virchow said omnis cellula e cellula.

119
00:05:11,988 --> 00:05:13,394
And Leeuwenhoek first saw a live cell.

120
00:05:13,994 --> 00:05:15,535
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:15,535 --> 00:05:17,119
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:20,319 --> 00:05:22,505
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

123
00:05:22,905 --> 00:05:23,805
One.

124
00:05:23,306 --> 00:05:25,579
What are the two rules of the cell theory?

125
00:05:26,479 --> 00:05:27,379
Two.

126
00:05:26,743 --> 00:05:27,752
What did Rudolf Virchow add?

127
00:05:28,652 --> 00:05:29,552
Three.

128
00:05:29,151 --> 00:05:30,704
Which is the smallest cell?

129
00:05:31,804 --> 00:05:35,571
Next time: a bacterium and
your cheek cell are both cells.

130
00:05:35,571 --> 00:05:37,556
So why are they so different?

131
00:05:37,556 --> 00:05:40,572
We find out in prokaryotic
and eukaryotic cells.
